WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.080
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.260
Today, I want to start this video with a new feature called: 
"The Mystery Language".

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.760
I'm going to play a language for you 
that you may have not heard before

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.780
and, at the end of the video, 
I will let you know what this language is.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:16.840
So check it out.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.440
Okay, stay tuned until the end of the video
and I will tell you what language you just heard.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.960
Okay, onto our main topic: 
What country has the most languages spoken?

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.960
What country is the most linguistically diverse?

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.860
Many of us come from countries where
there may just be one or two main languages,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.620
and maybe some other regional minority languages too.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:04.000
But there are some countries in the world where 
the amount of linguistic variation is just mind-blowing.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:11.020
The country with the most languages spoken is Papua-New Guinea 
with at least 820 different languages spoken.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.080
Imagine that!

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.580
Papua-New Guinea is a very rural country
where 88% of the people live in rural areas.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.260
Now compare that to 10% of the people 
in the United Kingdom living in rural areas,

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:26.880
or 19.3 % of the American population
living in rural areas.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.400
Papua-New Guinea is very rural indeed.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:37.280
And because of Papua New-Guinea's rugged terrain 
and its deep valleys, there's a limited amount of communication
between all of those isolated rural community.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.420
That's why the remains such an amazing 
amount of linguistic variety today,

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:46.320
without any particular languages sort of growing 
in influence and dominating the others.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:51.260
Imagine that your village speaks one language 
and the neighbouring village just a few kilometers away

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.580
speaks an entirely different language,
that's sometimes the case in Papua-New-Guinea.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:00.360
And I'm not even talking about different dialects
 of the same language, although that happens too.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:04.240
I'm talking about completely different languages 
in certain cases.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.320
The languages of Papua New Guinea 
fall into three categories :

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:13.420
The first one is the Austronesian languages which 
arrived in Papua New Guinea about 3,500 years ago.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:19.060
Then, there are the Papuan languages which predate 
the Austronesian languages in Papua New Guinea.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.720
And then, there are the foreign languages
and Creole languages that serve as a lingua francas.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:29.180
The term pop Papuan language simply refers to 
languages that predate the Austronesian languages,

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.840
languages that are indigenous to Papua New Guinea.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.520
It does not mean that they are a single language family.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.720
There are actually several dozen different language 
families amongst the Papuan languages.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.820
Some of those language families 
have no connection to each other.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.820
And there are some language isolates.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:49.560
That means that there are individual languages 
that have no known connection 
to any other languages in the world.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.200
Most Papuan languages are spoken by
only a few hundred to a few thousand people.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.600
Though, there are a few that are spoken
by over a hundred thousand people.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:04.380
There are three official languages in Papua New Guinea :
the first one is English, the second is Tok Pisin 
and the third one is Hiri Motu.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.500
The most widely spoken is Tok Pisin.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.520
It is an English creole language 
which arose during the colonial period.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:16.040
Tok Pisin is now used as a lingua franca which is very 
important in a country with so many different languages.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:19.480
And it is also now spoken 
as a native language by many people.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.960
Tok Pisin is less widely spoken in the southern region of Papua,

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.280
where a different language often functions
as the lingua franca. And that is Hiri Motu.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:32.380
Hiri Motu is a simplified Creole 
of another Austronesian language called Motu.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:36.720
Its use has been declining in recent times
in favor of English and Tok Pisin.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.940
English is only spoken by 1 to 2 % of the population 
but it is widely used in business

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.860
and it is supposedly the language of education.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:53.580
But if English is the language of education and only 
1 to 2% of the people speak it, then that sounds like 
a pretty dodgy education system to me.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:59.780
The second most linguistically diverse country in the world 
is Indonesia, which lies right next to Papua New Guinea.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:03.420
In fact, half of the island of New Guinea
lies inside indonesia.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.040
About 740 languages are spoken in Indonesia.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:13.200
But there is only one official language and lingua franca. 
And that is Bahasa Indonesia, or Indonesian.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:17.560
Number three is Nigeria with 516 languages spoken.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.580
But English is used as a lingua franca,
 especially in the urban areas

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:25.380
I guess they need English to communicate
with all those thieving oil companies.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.000
Number four is India with 427 languages.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:35.060
The main lingua francas are Hindi as well as English,
which was left behind by the British colonizers,

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.040
in exchange for a massively huge amount of India's resources.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.900
And number five is the USA which has 311 languages.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:47.600
Now, that may sound surprising because we think 
of the USA as an English-speaking country.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:55.580
But remember that there are over 100 indigenous 
languages in the USA, even though many of them have 
a very limited number of speakers these days.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.480
There are also a couple of hundred of different
languages spoken by immigrants to the USA.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:05.120
It may be hard to wrap your head around
that huge degree of linguistic diversity,

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.520
especially if you are interested in one of those countries

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.880
and you want to learn a language 
to communicate with people there.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.720
But luckily those countries all have one or more
lingua francas which can help you communicate

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:21.220
with a much larger proportion of the population 
from various different linguistic communities.

